  ["Laser trophectoderm biopsy fee", "Micro-surgical laboratory fee for cell extraction"],
  ["Genetic testing fee (per embryo)", "High-complexity NGS analysis for each embryo sampled"],
  ["Vitrification & FET cycle fee", "Cryopreservation, storage and endometrial preparation for transfer"],
];

const faqs = [
  ["Does embryo biopsy harm the future child?", "No. Laser biopsy takes only 5–10 cells from the outer placental layer of a blastocyst with 100+ cells and leaves the Inner Cell Mass untouched. Long-term outcome studies have not shown an increase in developmental problems."],
  ["Why is ICSI required for PGT instead of regular IVF?", "ICSI prevents contamination from extra sperm stuck to the egg shell, which could affect sequencing. It greatly reduces the chance of a false result from paternal DNA."],
  ["What is a mosaic embryo result?", "A mosaic embryo contains a mix of normal and abnormal cells. NGS can estimate the level of mosaicism, and some low-level mosaic embryos have led to healthy births. Dr. Kamran counsels you individually before any mosaic embryo is considered for transfer."],
  ["Can PGT guarantee a healthy pregnancy?", "No. NGS testing is about 98–99% accurate, but it screens the chromosomes or genes tested, not every possible condition. Uterine receptivity and other factors still matter, so routine prenatal care remains recommended."],
  ["Is PGT-A right for everyone?", "Not necessarily. Benefit is greatest with age 35+, recurrent loss or repeated implantation failure. For younger women with many embryos the benefit is less clear, so Dr. Kamran reviews your history before advising."],
  ["How long does the genetic result take?", "Reports usually take about 1–2 weeks. Biopsied embryos are vitrified during this time and transferred in a following frozen cycle."],
];

const treatments = [
